76
00:04:40,832 --> 00:04:43,948
Now, let's look at both of these
sentences carefully because

77
00:04:44,031 --> 00:04:45,364
neither of these is insincere.

78
00:04:45,842 --> 00:04:49,448
In 2024, these numbers
were real as reported.

79
00:04:49,861 --> 00:04:54,354
In 2025, they had lived with it long
enough to see what wasn't captured

80
00:04:55,354 --> 00:04:57,183
What is the lesson that
we can learn from this?

81
00:04:57,725 --> 00:05:01,838
The evaluation function that we
define is a product decision.

82
00:05:02,779 --> 00:05:06,292
Before starting any project, we need
to ensure that we choose this metrics

83
00:05:06,292 --> 00:05:10,827
carefully and track this metrics as the
project progresses to ensure that we are

84
00:05:10,855 --> 00:05:15,548
on track and building the right thing that
we intended to build With that context,

85
00:05:15,575 --> 00:05:19,980
now let's start going one by one into some
of the architectur-architectural decisions

86
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:21,161
that we have to make along the way.

87
00:05:22,338 --> 00:05:24,929
The first one, should you
build multi-agent systems?

88
00:05:26,605 --> 00:05:29,679
There's two sides to this argument,
and I want to talk about both of these.

89
00:05:30,551 --> 00:05:33,340
On the first side, here's
an example from Anthropic.

90
00:05:33,884 --> 00:05:37,819
They built a multi-agent research system,
an orchestrated lead researcher agent

91
00:05:37,832 --> 00:05:39,856
that spawns multiple parallel sub-agents.

92
00:05:40,389 --> 00:05:43,605
On their internal research
eval, it scored 90.2%

93
00:05:43,605 --> 00:05:45,402
higher than a single agent Opus.

94
00:05:46,059 --> 00:05:49,607
Parallel tool calls cut the
research time down by 90%.

95
00:05:50,214 --> 00:05:51,325
Now why does this work?

96
00:05:51,433 --> 00:05:52,976
Because the subtasks were read-only.

97
00:05:53,456 --> 00:05:58,291
Sub-agents can search the web, read pages,
and return findings in parallel, and that

98
00:05:58,291 --> 00:06:03,666
can drastically help the quality of the
response generated On the extreme opposite

99
00:06:03,695 --> 00:06:09,675
end, we have a blog post by Walden Yan
that says, "Don't build multiple agents."

100
00:06:10,015 --> 00:06:11,663
It lays out two principles.

101
00:06:12,758 --> 00:06:16,366
One, single-agent systems
can share the full context.

102
00:06:16,938 --> 00:06:20,914
Every part of the system needs a
complete picture, and by design,

103
00:06:20,918 --> 00:06:23,855
multiple-- multi-agent systems
will not be able to have this.

104
00:06:24,730 --> 00:06:29,638
The second argument in, in favor
of single-agent systems is that

105
00:06:29,666 --> 00:06:34,209
actions carry implicit decisions, and
confirs-- conflicting and implicit

106
00:06:34,226 --> 00:06:35,963
decisions compound into a conflict.

107
00:06:36,542 --> 00:06:40,799
Two agents writing in parallel are
two sets of uncoordinated decisions

108
00:06:40,803 --> 00:06:46,149
colliding, which inherently means that
multiple agent systems can have problems.

109
00:06:47,149 --> 00:06:52,693
Now, Cognition's own concession is
that Claude Code uses sub-agents

110
00:06:52,724 --> 00:06:56,514
successfully and very effectively
when the sub-tasks are read-only.

111
00:06:57,173 --> 00:07:01,099
Both sides agree on the mechanism, they
just disagree on where to draw the line.

112
00:07:02,621 --> 00:07:05,046
I think here's the decision rule
that I want to leave you with:

113
00:07:05,613 --> 00:07:09,144
Fan out only for read-only and
independently verifiable breadth.

114
00:07:09,553 --> 00:07:13,434
Write paths should sa-- should stay
single-threaded with full context.

115
00:07:13,856 --> 00:07:17,940
Sub-agents res-- return the compressed
findings, but never the full transcripts.

116
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:20,457
Now delegation patterns.

117
00:07:20,986 --> 00:07:24,541
If you do delegate work to multiple
agents, there are basically four shapes.

118
00:07:24,652 --> 00:07:28,207
Let's walk through each of them, and I'll
be talking through them in the clockwise

119
00:07:28,212 --> 00:07:29,539
direction as they appear on the screen.

120
00:07:30,548 --> 00:07:31,695
Supervisor architecture.

121
00:07:32,770 --> 00:07:36,375
An orchestrator looks at the task
and decides who does what on the

122
00:07:36,376 --> 00:07:41,420
fly The failure mode for this is
bottleneck and context bloating.

123
00:07:41,830 --> 00:07:46,473
Everything routes through one agent
and whose-- and the context window for

124
00:07:46,474 --> 00:07:49,855
that agent can fill up very quickly
with everyone else's transcript.

125
00:07:50,793 --> 00:07:51,866
What is the fix for this?

126
00:07:52,168 --> 00:07:56,831
We want to ensure that for every
delegation, there's a fixed turn and

127
00:07:56,980 --> 00:08:02,221
token budget, and the sub-agents are
required to only return compressed outputs

128
00:08:02,225 --> 00:08:03,928
instead of returning the full transcript.

129
00:08:04,926 --> 00:08:07,331
The second architecture is
the pipeline architecture.

130
00:08:08,437 --> 00:08:12,164
This is very effective in cases when
all the steps are known in advance.

131
00:08:12,517 --> 00:08:15,749
An agent passes a typed known
artifact down the chain.

132
00:08:16,509 --> 00:08:21,452
This is where most production value
lives today, and this is effectively

133
00:08:21,458 --> 00:08:25,287
because the steps are already known
beforehand and each hop is testable.

134
00:08:26,082 --> 00:08:28,840
The failure mode for this
one is drift poisoning.

135
00:08:29,915 --> 00:08:34,657
The garbage or, incorrect input that
flows into the system at the start of the

136
00:08:34,658 --> 00:08:39,632
pipeline flows all the way through and can
poison everything that happens downstream.

137
00:08:40,734 --> 00:08:44,826
A fix for this is to ensure that
there's a type contract between stages.

138
00:08:45,321 --> 00:08:48,929
We have mechanisms for retrying
from a checkpoint, dead letter

139
00:08:48,962 --> 00:08:52,481
queues, and ensuring that failed
items are handled effectively.

140
00:08:53,629 --> 00:08:57,359
The third architecture is a blackboard
architecture, where multiple agents

141
00:08:57,404 --> 00:09:02,205
attack the problem from different
corners and simultaneously post

142
00:09:02,224 --> 00:09:03,676
their findings to a shared state.

143
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:09,759
The failure mode for this one is in race
conditions while writing and potentially

144
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:15,535
duplication among agents, where two agents
could possibly tr-- possibly be trying to

145
00:09:15,535 --> 00:09:17,918
solve the same problem, multiple times.

146
00:09:18,563 --> 00:09:23,496
The fix for this is to ensure that we
have additive and version writes, and

147
00:09:23,506 --> 00:09:28,309
for the shared artifact, there's a lease
and quorum checks to ensure that, write,

148
00:09:28,393 --> 00:09:29,959
write race conditions do not happen.

149
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:34,730
And finally, we have the debate
architecture, where, agents are

150
00:09:35,094 --> 00:09:38,259
asked to argue with each other
and hold opposing points of view

151
00:09:39,413 --> 00:09:40,756
and present their arguments.

152
00:09:41,007 --> 00:09:44,657
A judge would then look at all, all the
arguments and pick the final winner.

153
00:09:45,539 --> 00:09:52,145
This is very powerful for verification,
but it fails to one of the most,

154
00:09:52,254 --> 00:09:55,496
well-known patterns within an
L- LLMs, which is sycophancy.

155
00:09:56,032 --> 00:10:00,422
Agents tend to agree very quickly, and
there's also a real cost and latency

156
00:10:00,425 --> 00:10:04,631
overhead since the agents can potentially
continue debi-debating for a long time.

157
00:10:05,605 --> 00:10:10,177
The fix for this is to ensure that
the role agents per-personas are

158
00:10:10,177 --> 00:10:14,330
genuinely adversarial, and there is
a cap on the number of rounds that

159
00:10:14,331 --> 00:10:18,004
the agents can debate before coming
up with a final response, and also

160
00:10:18,008 --> 00:10:22,933
having and setting predefined, human
escalation paths to ensure that an

161
00:10:22,934 --> 00:10:24,704
agent doesn't go into an infinite loop.

162
00:10:25,698 --> 00:10:30,569
Underneath all four of this sits what I
think I can call a delegation mechanism.

163
00:10:31,281 --> 00:10:34,797
Every delegation should have a contract
which includes a deadline, a token

164
00:10:34,798 --> 00:10:39,728
budget, and the response pattern
back, and this can be a structured

165
00:10:39,728 --> 00:10:42,467
summary or any predefined format.

166
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:48,100
Mutating steps should never fan out,
and there should always be a single

167
00:10:48,103 --> 00:10:49,649
named owner for the final answer

168
00:10:50,649 --> 00:10:53,727
Let's switch gears now, and we'll
talk about context engineering.

169
00:10:54,370 --> 00:10:55,418
Let's start with cost.

170
00:10:56,116 --> 00:10:59,864
Manus, the agent startup, published
their context engineering learnings last

171
00:10:59,866 --> 00:11:01,577
July, and the headline was striking.

172
00:11:02,285 --> 00:11:07,041
Their agent runs roughly 100 input
tokens for every output token, and

173
00:11:07,096 --> 00:11:08,650
this is a common pattern throughout.

174
00:11:08,708 --> 00:11:14,115
Input tokens vast the output tokens,
which makes one metric disproportionately

175
00:11:14,115 --> 00:11:15,796
important: cache hit rate.

176
00:11:16,670 --> 00:11:20,668
Manus calls it the single most important
metric for any AI agent company.

177
00:11:21,306 --> 00:11:22,703
Here's an experiment on the screen.

178
00:11:23,127 --> 00:11:25,631
Same 20-turn conversation,
two prompt layouts.

179
00:11:26,389 --> 00:11:27,769
First one is a stable layout.

180
00:11:28,296 --> 00:11:31,866
Big immutable block at the top,
which contains system prompt, tool

181
00:11:31,872 --> 00:11:36,157
definition, reference docs, et cetera,
followed by an append-only history.

182
00:11:36,827 --> 00:11:38,729
Volatile stuff pushed towards the end.

183
00:11:39,504 --> 00:11:41,910
The result is a 93% cache hit rate.

184
00:11:42,626 --> 00:11:44,503
The second layout is a volatile head.

185
00:11:45,095 --> 00:11:48,436
Timestamp or session ID is
near the top, and every turn

186
00:11:48,822 --> 00:11:50,273
invalidates everything above it.

187
00:11:50,786 --> 00:11:53,184
The result is a 33% hit rate.

188
00:11:54,226 --> 00:11:58,127
Now, this is the exact same model
for the exact same task, but with the

189
00:11:58,128 --> 00:12:02,577
stable layout, it's roughly six times
cheaper purely from the prompt layout.

190
00:12:03,664 --> 00:12:07,079
Cached tokens bill at around 10
times less than uncached tokens,

191
00:12:08,412 --> 00:12:09,946
and they serve faster too.

192
00:12:10,958 --> 00:12:14,594
Both of this means that your responses
with when you have a higher cache

193
00:12:14,597 --> 00:12:17,653
rate are both faster and cheaper.

194
00:12:18,656 --> 00:12:20,185
So what does this teach us?

195
00:12:20,230 --> 00:12:22,454
There are four rules that we can follow.

196
00:12:23,236 --> 00:12:28,348
We need to ensure that stable prompts
are available at top of the prompt block.

197
00:12:28,927 --> 00:12:32,689
This can include things like system
prompt, tools, documentation, et

198
00:12:32,690 --> 00:12:36,246
cetera, and this is basically everything
in the prompt that is immutable.

199
00:12:36,939 --> 00:12:41,674
Volatile data such as timestamps, session
IDs, user info should be appended to the

200
00:12:41,675 --> 00:12:43,389
end of the prompt, never at the head.

201
00:12:44,392 --> 00:12:48,580
And we want to ensure that
prompts are append-only history.

202
00:12:49,027 --> 00:12:51,329
We shouldn't be rewriting
the previous turns.

203
00:12:51,792 --> 00:12:55,804
And finally, wherever possible, we
should be masking unavailable tools

204
00:12:56,641 --> 00:12:59,232
using logits rather than removing them.

205
00:12:59,779 --> 00:13:04,120
Removal would shift the byte positions
and would impact the cache hit rate.

206
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:07,623
Context window is one of
the most important resources

207
00:13:07,623 --> 00:13:08,652
that we need to manage.

208
00:13:09,007 --> 00:13:12,187
When the context window fills,
practitioners have converged

209
00:13:12,187 --> 00:13:13,513
on four different solutions.

210
00:13:14,297 --> 00:13:15,886
First one is to compact.

211
00:13:16,263 --> 00:13:18,968
When history exceeds the
token budget, we compress it.

212
00:13:19,645 --> 00:13:24,205
We regenerate it from the artifacts
rather than doing a naive truncation.

213
00:13:24,725 --> 00:13:28,533
Naive truncation can potentially lose
constraints, and the model can forget

214
00:13:28,569 --> 00:13:30,212
what it was trying to do mid-task.

215
00:13:31,006 --> 00:13:34,255
The second, Approach is note-taking.

216
00:13:34,651 --> 00:13:38,671
Persistent findings are saved
outside the window in a note.md

217
00:13:38,681 --> 00:13:40,049
file or something like that.

218
00:13:40,922 --> 00:13:43,600
The agent keeps a running
file of everything it learned.

219
00:13:44,142 --> 00:13:47,170
It rereads this file regardless
of the conversation length.

220
00:13:47,639 --> 00:13:49,330
Claude Code does something very similar.

221
00:13:51,215 --> 00:13:52,826
The third pattern is recite.

222
00:13:53,762 --> 00:13:58,271
Rewrite the agent's objective into
the recent context every few turn.

223
00:13:58,796 --> 00:14:03,403
This fights the lost in the middle
pattern that most models see when

224
00:14:03,559 --> 00:14:08,999
they're left unattended over long
context Manus credits recitation for

225
00:14:09,051 --> 00:14:12,078
coherence across over 50 turn tool calls.

226
00:14:13,141 --> 00:14:15,247
And finally, we have
the quarantine pattern.

227
00:14:15,796 --> 00:14:21,914
Respond a sub-agent for every,
every independent task and

228
00:14:21,914 --> 00:14:23,664
return only the final conclusion.

229
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:29,230
The main thread pays only for the answer,
not for the analysis Notice that this is

230
00:14:29,233 --> 00:14:33,670
very similar to the previously discussed
read-only fan-out rule from earlier.

231
00:14:35,204 --> 00:14:39,025
Let's now talk about reliability, and
the core idea fits in one formula.

232
00:14:39,673 --> 00:14:44,511
For a n-step chain at a per step
reliability of r, the end-to-end

233
00:14:44,513 --> 00:14:46,758
success rate is r raised to the power n.

234
00:14:47,628 --> 00:14:53,556
So for example, for 10 steps at a
reliability per step of 99%, the

235
00:14:53,590 --> 00:14:55,903
total chain reliability is 90%.

236
00:14:56,661 --> 00:15:03,682
This drops to 35% reliability for a
chain whose per step reliability is 90%.

237
00:15:05,395 --> 00:15:06,039
Read that again.

238
00:15:06,247 --> 00:15:11,965
A 95% reliability per step sounds
great, but as the number of steps

239
00:15:11,965 --> 00:15:16,619
increases, the reliability of the entire
system keeps dropping exponentially.

240
00:15:17,913 --> 00:15:18,975
But here's the good news.

241
00:15:19,246 --> 00:15:21,675
This is something that
we can easily address.

242
00:15:22,757 --> 00:15:25,421
There are a few things that
are recommended to ensure

243
00:15:25,470 --> 00:15:29,212
that the agent carries its
reliability across long chains.

244
00:15:30,510 --> 00:15:33,593
First one is to have retries
with backoff and jitter.

245
00:15:34,873 --> 00:15:39,126
The second one is to ensure that
there are fallback and degraded paths.

246
00:15:39,644 --> 00:15:44,555
When the primary path fails, we should
have systems in place that can degrade

247
00:15:44,555 --> 00:15:48,500
effectively and gracefully instead of just
dying or cascading errors down the lane.

248
00:15:49,776 --> 00:15:52,668
We should also include circuit
breakers and timeouts to ensure

249
00:15:52,700 --> 00:15:56,249
that there are no cascading failures
and to stop any hanging waits.

250
00:15:56,898 --> 00:16:00,743
And finally, we should have
idempotency keys on mutations.

251
00:16:01,050 --> 00:16:03,681
Without them, a retry
would be a double delete.

252
00:16:04,599 --> 00:16:08,597
Retries can only help if ref-repeating
an action is a safe thing to do

253
00:16:09,597 --> 00:16:13,333
Remember the Replit story, the
fabricated users, the fake test reports?

254
00:16:14,162 --> 00:16:16,721
This slide is that story
drawn as a system diagram.

255
00:16:17,481 --> 00:16:19,850
The hallucination loop has four steps.

256
00:16:20,395 --> 00:16:23,514
One, a small factual
error enters the context.

257
00:16:23,730 --> 00:16:26,143
Two, the plan gets built
on top of that error.

258
00:16:26,597 --> 00:16:32,139
Three, tool results re-- get read
as the confirmation of the error.

259
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:36,133
Four, confidence rises
when divergence compounds.

260
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:39,781
The loop escalated the problem.

261
00:16:41,072 --> 00:16:42,687
Let's look at the
trajectories on the screen.

262
00:16:43,306 --> 00:16:50,047
The unguarded runs diverge smoothly, and
the note at the disclaimer indicates this.

263
00:16:50,744 --> 00:16:52,162
There are three structural breaks.

264
00:16:53,589 --> 00:16:57,861
Implementing this will ensure that the
agent's hallucination does not cascade.

265
00:16:58,707 --> 00:16:59,409
Grounding it.

266
00:16:59,810 --> 00:17:04,262
Every claim that the agent makes must
be cited with retrieved context, or

267
00:17:04,771 --> 00:17:06,332
the claim should not be validated.

268
00:17:07,634 --> 00:17:11,831
Any uncited claim should be considered
unsupported, blocked, or flagged.

269
00:17:12,833 --> 00:17:13,528
Validation.

270
00:17:14,036 --> 00:17:19,089
Schema sanity checks should be done
on every to-- every tool output

271
00:17:19,411 --> 00:17:20,738
before they enter the context.

272
00:17:21,244 --> 00:17:25,471
Bad JSON, impossible values, or
anything that can be caught by

273
00:17:25,471 --> 00:17:28,911
code in a deterministic manner
should be caught by code and ensure

274
00:17:28,927 --> 00:17:30,227
that it does not flow downstream.

275
00:17:31,277 --> 00:17:35,301
Finally, a slightly more costly
but effective method is to have

276
00:17:35,302 --> 00:17:36,622
a critic pass over the plan.

277
00:17:37,378 --> 00:17:43,476
This can help ensure that an agent
catches any drift effectively before the

278
00:17:43,477 --> 00:17:45,178
drift affects the downstream systems.

279
00:17:46,178 --> 00:17:47,942
There is one counterintuitive practice.

280
00:17:49,074 --> 00:17:53,686
A lot of recommendations nowadays
mention pro-- mention retaining the

281
00:17:53,687 --> 00:17:55,522
failures in the context deliberately.

282
00:17:56,292 --> 00:18:00,264
This ensures that the agent knows
what it did wrong and prevents it

283
00:18:00,265 --> 00:18:01,888
from repeating the same mistake again

284
00:18:02,888 --> 00:18:06,458
There's a final section that I want
to talk about, which is the cost

285
00:18:07,458 --> 00:18:11,031
Multi-agent research systems, like
we discussed before, cost about 15

286
00:18:11,032 --> 00:18:17,753
times more tokens and can potentially
become very expensive very quickly.

287
00:18:18,314 --> 00:18:22,491
Tokens are simultaneously our
quality dial, but also our cost dial.

288
00:18:23,198 --> 00:18:28,063
So there are three levers in roughly
the, return on investment order

289
00:18:29,580 --> 00:18:32,552
that we can use to ensure that
we use tokens more effectively.

290
00:18:33,273 --> 00:18:38,235
Lever one is routing the
agent to the right LLM.

291
00:18:39,282 --> 00:18:43,158
Classify incoming requests based
on complexity and ensure that

292
00:18:43,233 --> 00:18:48,063
the simpler requests are sent to
cheaper models, and preserve hard

293
00:18:48,069 --> 00:18:49,946
things to the expensive models

294
00:18:50,946 --> 00:18:53,077
Layer two is caching and reusing.

295
00:18:54,084 --> 00:19:00,757
Manus' measured approximately ten X on
their input pricing by saving cache The

296
00:19:00,758 --> 00:19:03,482
second lever is caching and reusing.

297
00:19:05,390 --> 00:19:10,544
Almost 10x the price can be saved
by, using cache effectively using

298
00:19:10,546 --> 00:19:11,959
the methods that we discussed before.

299
00:19:13,088 --> 00:19:15,574
Level three is governing.

300
00:19:16,323 --> 00:19:19,574
We need to ensure that there
is proper monitoring and

301
00:19:19,574 --> 00:19:23,712
governance of, of token usage.

302
00:19:24,307 --> 00:19:28,640
This could include things like per team
quotas, per run budget caps enforced by

303
00:19:28,640 --> 00:19:33,339
the orchestrator, admission control during
peaks, batch windows for asynchronous

304
00:19:33,353 --> 00:19:39,429
work, and more-- most importantly, a kill
switch to ensure that an agent or an LLM

305
00:19:39,444 --> 00:19:42,333
can be stopped immediately when desired.

306
00:19:43,391 --> 00:19:47,549
The skill-- kill switch can be
considered a safety feature, but

307
00:19:47,550 --> 00:19:51,708
it can also be considered as a
load-bearing infrastructure to ensure

308
00:19:51,740 --> 00:19:58,220
that the load on LLMs does not, hit
more than the allowed capability.

309
00:19:59,322 --> 00:20:02,890
When something goes wrong at scale, there
needs to be a button that stops spend in

310
00:20:02,890 --> 00:20:07,809
seconds, not a config change that would
need to be reviewed and deployed and

311
00:20:07,810 --> 00:20:09,980
go through the typical CI/CD process.

312
00:20:11,511 --> 00:20:13,364
Let's assemble everything
into one picture.

313
00:20:13,545 --> 00:20:16,662
There are nine layers, which I'm
calling the control plane of a

314
00:20:16,662 --> 00:20:18,325
production, production agent system.

315
00:20:18,776 --> 00:20:22,898
Each layer exists because it
answers one question that the

316
00:20:22,907 --> 00:20:24,661
POC never had a chance to answer.

317
00:20:26,344 --> 00:20:28,181
Gateway and authorization.

318
00:20:28,397 --> 00:20:31,165
This includes rate limits,
quotas, and identity.

319
00:20:31,787 --> 00:20:34,859
This answers the question:
Who is allowed to spend money?

320
00:20:35,974 --> 00:20:41,733
Orchestrator does the planning, retries
timeouts, and ensures budget control.

321
00:20:42,469 --> 00:20:45,696
This answers the question: Who
enforces the contracts we keep writing?

322
00:20:47,054 --> 00:20:51,069
Then we have the agent fleet, which
includes the specialized workers, sandbox

323
00:20:51,069 --> 00:20:55,393
executors, and answers the question:
Where does the actual work run safely?

324
00:20:56,393 --> 00:20:57,928
Level four is a tool layer.

325
00:20:58,445 --> 00:21:03,790
This includes the typed contracts, circuit
breakers, idempotency keys, and can be,

326
00:21:03,832 --> 00:21:10,558
used to answer the question: What happens
when the outward behavior is unexpected?

327
00:21:11,948 --> 00:21:17,022
We have memory and state, which includes
things like vector stores, session states,

328
00:21:17,061 --> 00:21:21,234
checkpoints, and can be used to answer
the question: What does the system do

329
00:21:21,309 --> 00:21:23,387
to survive restarts and share context?

330
00:21:25,148 --> 00:21:29,648
We have the observability layer, which
tracks the traces, tokens, cost summaries.

331
00:21:31,209 --> 00:21:33,850
It can be used to replay
conversations, and so on.

332
00:21:34,498 --> 00:21:38,237
This can be used to answer the
question of understanding what

333
00:21:38,248 --> 00:21:39,623
happened when something breaks.

334
00:21:40,497 --> 00:21:44,410
We have an eval harness, which includes
the golden datasets, LLM as judges,

335
00:21:44,457 --> 00:21:51,677
and regression gates to help understand
and ensure that the future performance

336
00:21:51,681 --> 00:21:55,104
is always better than the current
performance, and the agent continuously--

337
00:21:55,786 --> 00:21:59,198
We have the required inputs to ensure
that the agent continuously improves.

338
00:22:00,184 --> 00:22:05,464
We have deploy and fallback, which
includes canary percentages, shadow mode

339
00:22:05,466 --> 00:22:07,324
deployment, instant reverts, and so on.

340
00:22:07,403 --> 00:22:10,738
We'll be talking more about this in
just a minute, but these are some of the

341
00:22:10,738 --> 00:22:14,694
typical practices used to ensure that the
agent is deployed safely and effectively.

342
00:22:15,499 --> 00:22:19,564
Finally, we have guardrails, which can
include things like PII filters, policy

343
00:22:19,564 --> 00:22:25,256
engine, human-in-the-loop queues to
ensure that the agent is performing

344
00:22:25,266 --> 00:22:27,700
effectively even in production.

345
00:22:28,115 --> 00:22:32,179
And when it does have a miss, that
miss is surfaced to the user as

346
00:22:32,180 --> 00:22:36,761
quickly as possible Let's talk a
little bit more about the evaluation.

347
00:22:37,292 --> 00:22:40,551
How do we test something that is
fundamentally non-deterministic?

348
00:22:41,476 --> 00:22:42,948
The answer is in tiers.

349
00:22:43,774 --> 00:22:47,591
We have multiple layers of
testing, each one testing one

350
00:22:47,594 --> 00:22:49,142
part of the system systematically.

351
00:22:50,253 --> 00:22:53,359
The first layer is the
deterministic contract testing.

352
00:22:54,132 --> 00:22:58,346
In this, we test for things
like is the output schema valid?

353
00:22:58,834 --> 00:23:01,346
Are there any-- Are all the
required fields present?

354
00:23:01,581 --> 00:23:03,193
Is there any arguments?

355
00:23:03,397 --> 00:23:06,649
If so, are the arguments
within the desired values?

356
00:23:07,719 --> 00:23:10,175
Are there any PII values
leaked, and so on.

357
00:23:11,190 --> 00:23:15,427
We-- This will be the pure code
tests that are run as part of CI/CD

358
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:16,983
and can be used to block merges.

359
00:23:18,390 --> 00:23:20,886
The second tier is LLM as judges.

360
00:23:21,424 --> 00:23:26,022
We have a golden data set that
is defined, and the a-agent

361
00:23:26,054 --> 00:23:27,485
is scored based on a rubric.

362
00:23:27,920 --> 00:23:32,295
Judges correlate this with the per--
human performance as part of calibration

363
00:23:32,812 --> 00:23:37,221
and ensure that this test is run as
part of CI/CD for every deployment.

364
00:23:39,148 --> 00:23:41,926
Simulation and canaries run
against a staging world.

365
00:23:42,375 --> 00:23:45,420
Automatic rollback can be done
whenever there's a guardrail breach.

366
00:23:45,929 --> 00:23:49,236
And in the last level, we
have a human test, which would

367
00:23:49,271 --> 00:23:51,272
include expert review queues.

368
00:23:52,010 --> 00:23:55,895
This is done to ensure that any crucial
feedback flows back from the golden

369
00:23:55,895 --> 00:23:59,232
data set and from the production
system back into the machine, so

370
00:23:59,234 --> 00:24:02,190
that the machine learns from these
reviewers instead of just consuming

371
00:24:02,208 --> 00:24:05,213
them and repeating the mistakes
over and over again in the future.

372
00:24:06,821 --> 00:24:10,487
A key thing to remember from all this
is that if a change is not covered

373
00:24:10,503 --> 00:24:12,074
by an eval, it isn't a change.

374
00:24:12,477 --> 00:24:13,753
It is a coin flip.

375
00:24:14,356 --> 00:24:18,679
There should not be any code change
that is made without an eval, and

376
00:24:18,783 --> 00:24:22,541
any merge or deployment should be
blocked until it is properly evaluated

377
00:24:23,541 --> 00:24:23,989
interchange.

378
00:24:24,309 --> 00:24:25,569
It is a coin flip.

379
00:24:26,190 --> 00:24:30,520
There should not be any code change
that is made without an eval, and

380
00:24:30,626 --> 00:24:34,365
any merge or deployment should be
blocked until it is properly evaluated.

381
00:24:35,239 --> 00:24:36,727
Finally, delivery discipline.

382
00:24:37,247 --> 00:24:41,956
Delivery discipline is one of the things
that is the most important parts to ensure

383
00:24:41,974 --> 00:24:44,274
that the agent works well in production.

384
00:24:44,943 --> 00:24:47,736
Agents ship like software
because they are software.

385
00:24:48,441 --> 00:24:52,321
They are just a little bit weirder
in terms of inputs and have

386
00:24:53,310 --> 00:24:55,279
potentially non-deterministic behavior

387
00:24:56,279 --> 00:24:59,602
In the promotion path for an agent,
there are typically four stages.

388
00:25:00,034 --> 00:25:04,306
There's the offline evals, and
we need to ensure that any new

389
00:25:04,329 --> 00:25:08,749
deployment should meet the existing
baseline, as a criteria for promotion.

390
00:25:09,538 --> 00:25:12,410
Then we have a shadow traffic,
which mirrors the real request,

391
00:25:12,468 --> 00:25:15,409
but the outputs are discarded
and used only for evaluation.

392
00:25:15,927 --> 00:25:21,672
This helps ensure that there is zero user
risk, and the agent is tested on inputs

393
00:25:21,672 --> 00:25:23,626
that look like production-related inputs.

394
00:25:24,635 --> 00:25:29,364
We have canary deployments to have an
increasing rollout of traffic that can

395
00:25:29,391 --> 00:25:31,408
auto revert whenever there's a regression.

396
00:25:32,091 --> 00:25:35,962
This could include things like
ensuring that only 1% of the traffic

397
00:25:35,964 --> 00:25:40,510
reaches a new agent, and this slowly
rolls up to full traffic as the

398
00:25:40,510 --> 00:25:42,122
performance is constantly monitored.

399
00:25:42,777 --> 00:25:46,431
And finally, during the full rollout,
we need to ensure that there's a

400
00:25:46,437 --> 00:25:52,827
SLO dashboard, error budget, and an
on-call to prevent any issues from

401
00:25:52,828 --> 00:25:55,441
happening in production and resolve
them as they occ-as they occur.

402
00:25:56,593 --> 00:25:59,758
We also need to have rollback triggers
which are automated and wired to

403
00:25:59,761 --> 00:26:03,042
guardrails so that, regression
doesn't wait for a human to correct.

404
00:26:03,763 --> 00:26:08,275
There-- Some examples of the guardrails
could be things like groundedness drops

405
00:26:08,275 --> 00:26:16,269
below 5%, of the baseline, tool errors
are 10% more than the baseline, and so on.

406
00:26:16,584 --> 00:26:20,965
There can also be cost and latency
related, related baselines or

407
00:26:20,994 --> 00:26:22,857
guardrails that can trigger a rollback.

408
00:26:23,857 --> 00:26:26,166
We've covered a lot, so
here's a compressed version.

409
00:26:26,312 --> 00:26:27,780
Eight things to do before you scale.

410
00:26:28,967 --> 00:26:30,523
Name two numbers before scaling.

411
00:26:30,927 --> 00:26:33,903
These are the end-to-end success
rate, the cost per completed

412
00:26:33,935 --> 00:26:37,743
task, and monitor this as part of
your development and deployment.

413
00:26:38,369 --> 00:26:44,683
Pick orchestration by task, task
shape, and this is the criteria of

414
00:26:44,742 --> 00:26:48,622
multiple agents versus single agent
that we were talking of earlier.

415
00:26:49,521 --> 00:26:52,263
Every delegation should require
a contract, and choose the

416
00:26:52,265 --> 00:26:53,786
delegation contract effectively.

417
00:26:55,361 --> 00:26:59,933
Plan for cache hit rates to ensure that
you're using the cache effectively.

418
00:27:00,573 --> 00:27:05,844
Ensure that the agent has reliability
control set in it, which can include

419
00:27:05,847 --> 00:27:10,471
things like retries with backoff
and jitter, fallbacks, br-- circuit

420
00:27:10,471 --> 00:27:12,497
breakers, timeout, and idempotency keys.

421
00:27:13,301 --> 00:27:18,073
Break hallucination loop structurally
by having fail closed, grounding,

422
00:27:18,150 --> 00:27:19,622
validation gates, and so on.

423
00:27:20,947 --> 00:27:22,181
Ship behind gates.

424
00:27:22,569 --> 00:27:27,912
There should be a CI/CD or a automatic
tiered eval that runs for every

425
00:27:27,912 --> 00:27:31,339
deployment, along with a provision
to automatically roll back when

426
00:27:31,342 --> 00:27:32,766
certain guardrails are breached.

427
00:27:33,303 --> 00:27:36,412
And finally, own the evaluation function.

428
00:27:36,742 --> 00:27:41,477
Decide what the system optimizes for,
or it can optimize for unexpected

429
00:27:41,484 --> 00:27:42,987
outcomes which may not be intended.

430
00:27:43,987 --> 00:27:44,958
That was my talk.

431
00:27:45,162 --> 00:27:45,966
Thank you everyone.
